Quite clearly how?  Define "god."  

The Valar are obviously more powerful than angels, as they each have
control over some aspect of the world, or the ability to fashion the
world within that aspect.  Elbereth, at the least, is worshipped.
They're closer to a Pantheon than to a group of angels.

Let's check some definitions:

      any supernatural being worshipped as controlling some part
      of the world or some aspect of life or who is the
      personification of a force
                     
      A being conceived of as possessing supernatural power, and
      to be propitiated by sacrifice, worship, etc.; a divinity;
      a deity; an object of worship; an idol.

When you're given extremely powerful supernatural entities with power
over specific aspects, like wind, water, earth, death, plants, light,
etc, it's reasonable to call them gods.  The Romans did it, after all.

The fact that their powers are restricted is moot.  Every Roman god was
restricted in some way.  Even Jupiter had no power over Terminus, yet he
was still a god.  

